         <div type="edition" xml:lang="tel-Latn">
            <p>
              <lb n="1"/>svasti śrī cōḻamahā<lb n="2" break="no"/>rājull<choice><orig>ī</orig><reg>ē</reg></choice>ḷaN ErigaL
              <lb n="3"/>dugarājuL Iccina pa<lb n="4" break="no"/><unclear>n</unclear>nāsa kocciya pāṟa <unclear>r</unclear>ē<lb n="5" break="no"/>vasarmmārikiN tēni ḻa<lb n="6" break="no"/>ccinavānṟu pañcamahā<lb n="7" break="no"/>pātakasaṁyyuktunṟu <supplied reason="omitted">A</supplied>gu
              <lb n="8"/><unclear>As</unclear>ibhairuvu likhita<supplied reason="lost">ṁ</supplied>
            </p>
         </div>
         <div type="apparatus">
               <listApp>
                  <app loc="2">
                     <lem>°rājull<choice><orig>ī</orig><reg>ē</reg></choice>ḷaN</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">°rājullēḷaN</rdg>
                     <note>The akṣara 〈llī〉 shows the usual form of the long 〈ī〉 on top of 〈l〉. While this spelling could reflect a dialectal pecularity of the place and time (<foreign>īḷan</foreign> instead of <foreign>ēḷan</foreign>), a similar spelling of 〈le〉 appears in the Aihoḷe inscription of the reign of Pulakeśin II (<bibl><ptr target="bib:Fleet1879_02"/></bibl>).</note>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="2">
                     <lem>ErigaL</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">Eriga<unclear>L</unclear></rdg>
                     <note>The helanta 〈L〉 differs from 〈la〉 in that the right vertical stroke that normally would bow towards the left over the akṣara stops as a straight vertical line and thus sort of resembles the la-characters of the 2nd and 3rd c. AD.</note>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="3">
                     <lem>pa<lb n="4" break="no"/><unclear>n</unclear>nāsa</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">pa<lb n="4" break="no"/><unclear>n</unclear>nasa</rdg>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="4">
                     <lem><unclear>r</unclear>ē<lb n="5" break="no"/>vasarmmārikiN</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01"><unclear>re</unclear><lb n="5" break="no"/>va<choice><orig>s</orig><reg>ś</reg></choice>armmārikiN</rdg>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="7">
                     <lem>saṁyyuktunṟu <supplied reason="omitted">A</supplied>gu</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">saṁyyuktunṟugu</rdg>
                     <note>Confer °pātakun<supplied reason="lost">ṟ</supplied>u Agu (<bibl><ptr target="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01"/><citedRange unit="page">228-229</citedRange><citedRange unit="item">42 C</citedRange><citedRange unit="line">8</citedRange></bibl>).</note>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="8">
                     <lem><unclear>As</unclear>ibhairuvu</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">Asivairuvu</rdg>
                     <note>Since /bh/ in skt. words merges with /v/ in a lot instances in Telugu (<foreign>vallabha</foreign>- ~ <foreign>vallava</foreign>-) both °<foreign>bhairuvu</foreign> and °<foreign>vairuvu</foreign> would go back to skt. <foreign>bhairava</foreign>-. The phonological realization in Telugu (°<foreign>bhairuvu</foreign>) could have synchronically been interpreted as corresponding to skt. u-stems that obtain the Telugu ending -vu (as <foreign>guruvu</foreign>). That may be the reason why there is no suffix -nṟu.</note>
                  </app>
                  <app loc="8">
                     <lem>likhita<supplied reason="lost">ṁ</supplied></lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01">likhitaṁ</rdg>
                  </app>
               </listApp>         
         </div>
         <div type="translation" source="bib:Sastri1969_01">
            <p>
               Hail! Prosperity! While Cōḻa-Mahārāja was ruling, Erigal-Dugarāja gave a pannasa to Rēvaśarmma, a brahmin of Kocciya (i. e. Kauśika gotra). He who obstructs (the gift) will incur the five great sins. (This is) written by Asivairuvu.
            </p>
         </div>
         <div type="translation" resp="part:jeth">
 	        <p>
 	           Svasti! A pannasa given by Erigal Dugarāju to the brahmin Rēvasarmma of Kocci while Śrī Cōḻamahārāju was ruling. Who (so ever) annuls it deserves to have (the outcome of) the five great sins. Written (by) Asibhairuvu. <!-- The translation 'deserves to have' might be quite good insofar it renders both meanings of yukta-, that are attested in Te as well, namely 'being fit or worthy of sth' and 'being connected to something'. It also sort of conveys the non-past agu. -->
 	        </p>
         </div>
         <div type="commentary">
            <p>
               
            </p>
         </div>
        <div type="bibliography">
          <p>
           The inscription was noted in A. R. No. 310 of 1935-1936 <!-- has to be adjusted to the @target --> and first edited by Nilakanta Sastri and Venkataramayya with a picture and annotations (<bibl rend="omitname"><ptr target="bib:NilakantaSastri+Venkataramayya1947-1948_01"/><citedRange unit="page">229-230</citedRange><citedRange unit="item">42 D</citedRange></bibl>). Based on this edition K. M. Sastri re-edited the text and provided a translation (<bibl rend="omitname"><ptr target="bib:Sastri1969_01"/><citedRange unit="page">284</citedRange><citedRange unit="item">7</citedRange></bibl>).
          </p>
